WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.960
We've talked a lot about props.

00:02.960 --> 00:05.280
We talked about emits.

00:05.280 --> 00:06.960
And so something you might be thinking, though,

00:06.960 --> 00:10.720
is that, well, OK, this is my mixed sense, right?

00:10.720 --> 00:13.680
I can pass data down to my components.

00:13.680 --> 00:17.840
But at some point, this might get cumbersome.

00:17.840 --> 00:21.560
And so I want to give you a microversion of the button

00:21.560 --> 00:25.600
exercise that I give in the production grade view workshop.

00:25.600 --> 00:27.760
So if you want to check that out for this.

00:27.800 --> 00:30.760
But let's, for a second, think of a component.

00:30.760 --> 00:35.440
Let's go ahead and depict this out real quick as a base button.

00:35.440 --> 00:38.720
And so for this base button, you probably

00:38.720 --> 00:41.560
are thinking, well, it's a button component.

00:41.560 --> 00:44.520
And what kind of props would I want to pass to it?

00:44.520 --> 00:50.000
Well, if we think about it, we can say, well, usually, you

00:50.000 --> 00:51.640
need to have some text on your button.

00:51.640 --> 00:55.120
So OK, let's call that a text prop.

00:55.160 --> 00:59.560
And then I'll do the shorthand typing right now.

00:59.560 --> 01:01.600
So this is an alternative, by the way, for props,

01:01.600 --> 01:03.720
where if you don't want to do the required in default,

01:03.720 --> 01:05.640
you just pass the type directly like this.

01:05.640 --> 01:07.080
But again, generally speaking, I think

01:07.080 --> 01:09.480
it's the best practice to define whether it's required

01:09.480 --> 01:11.480
or if it's not required and has a default.

01:11.480 --> 01:13.160
Basically, do your due diligence on that.

01:13.160 --> 01:15.200
It helps your team in the long run.

01:15.200 --> 01:19.240
OK, so you're like, OK, well, there might be some text here.

01:19.240 --> 01:23.040
And then what happens if your product manager is like,

01:23.040 --> 01:25.760
you know what, I think I want to use the icons on the left-hand

01:25.760 --> 01:26.260
side.

01:26.260 --> 01:30.720
Then you're like, oh, OK, maybe icon left string.

01:30.720 --> 01:31.720
Or maybe just icon, right?

01:31.720 --> 01:33.440
Because you're like, oh, OK, it has an icon.

01:33.440 --> 01:34.280
It has an icon.

01:34.280 --> 01:36.960
It's going to the left.

01:36.960 --> 01:39.920
And then they go, well, actually,

01:39.920 --> 01:43.360
it depends because the icon might be on the left or the right.

01:43.360 --> 01:45.160
And then you might start getting the hint

01:45.160 --> 01:45.880
of where I'm going with this.

01:45.880 --> 01:47.440
But then you're like, OK, well, icon right.

01:47.440 --> 01:48.880
And for now, I'm just keeping it simple,

01:48.880 --> 01:50.600
just keeping these strings.

01:50.640 --> 01:52.360
So I was like, OK, string.

01:55.160 --> 01:57.360
And then all of a sudden, it's like, well, I also

01:57.360 --> 02:00.240
need this to replace a loading spinner in case it's

02:00.240 --> 02:01.760
like fetching an API call.

02:01.760 --> 02:03.400
And all of a sudden, what you got here

02:03.400 --> 02:07.000
is this massive list of props for what is really

02:07.000 --> 02:10.000
just a button component, especially for something

02:10.000 --> 02:11.040
like this text here.

02:11.040 --> 02:14.200
Can you imagine how complicated the v if else if is going to be

02:14.200 --> 02:17.760
when it's like, if loading, if loading shows this, but else

02:17.760 --> 02:19.440
this, but then also if there's an icon,

02:19.440 --> 02:23.120
then do this, this button will become very, very troublesome

02:23.120 --> 02:25.240
to manage.

02:25.240 --> 02:29.720
And so of course, Vue has an answer for this.

02:29.720 --> 02:34.240
And the answer for this is around the idea of slots.

02:34.240 --> 02:36.720
And so slots are an interesting concept

02:36.720 --> 02:39.600
in that we have been using them this whole time when

02:39.600 --> 02:41.160
it comes to HTML.

02:41.160 --> 02:42.520
So how does a slot work?

02:42.520 --> 02:44.520
Well, let's go ahead and just show you how.

02:44.520 --> 02:45.920
Let's just refactor this real quick.

02:45.920 --> 02:52.680
So icon, let's do this, icon text.

02:52.680 --> 02:54.920
So I just want to have something that will render on the page.

02:57.560 --> 03:02.400
And then we'll say, OK, so let's go.

03:02.400 --> 03:03.720
Actually, since this is a playground,

03:03.720 --> 03:05.920
let me move the base button to playground

03:05.920 --> 03:07.760
so that this can be consistent.

03:07.760 --> 03:12.120
Base button is going to source, going to components.

03:12.120 --> 03:12.640
There we go.

03:12.640 --> 03:13.680
Perfect.

03:13.680 --> 03:14.960
And then to keep that consistent,

03:14.960 --> 03:18.400
we'll just do base button in kebab case.

03:18.400 --> 03:20.160
We'll hide that.

03:20.160 --> 03:23.920
And then now let's make sure I'm running the playground app

03:23.920 --> 03:26.440
so we don't run the wrong one.

03:26.440 --> 03:27.840
Because nothing's more infuriating

03:27.840 --> 03:29.840
than thinking being in the wrong environment

03:29.840 --> 03:32.760
and thinking your code's not changing.

03:32.760 --> 03:36.560
OK, so we have a base button here, app.vue.

03:36.560 --> 03:47.960
So import base button, base button from here, base and button.

03:47.960 --> 03:51.800
And then this is supposed to be base counter.

03:51.800 --> 03:54.960
OK, how does base button work at the moment?

03:54.960 --> 03:56.800
It is base button.

03:56.800 --> 04:03.560
And we can give it a text of hi and the icon of arrow.

04:03.560 --> 04:05.520
OK, so what happened?

04:05.520 --> 04:08.800
Oh, I think I did not close it.

04:08.800 --> 04:09.840
There we go.

04:09.840 --> 04:11.160
OK, we see it here, right?

04:11.160 --> 04:14.160
It says arrow and hi.

04:14.160 --> 04:16.280
Great.

04:16.280 --> 04:19.560
What we want to avoid when it comes to props

04:19.560 --> 04:21.760
is being overly descriptive with how something

04:21.760 --> 04:22.840
is supposed to be used.

04:22.840 --> 04:24.160
It's useful for guidance.

04:24.160 --> 04:26.160
It's useful as a guardrail.

04:26.160 --> 04:29.360
But when it comes to something like what text belongs in a button,

04:29.360 --> 04:32.720
it doesn't often make sense to do something like that with a prop.

04:32.720 --> 04:35.040
So instead, what do we do?

04:35.040 --> 04:39.000
Well, there is a built-in element called slot.

04:41.880 --> 04:44.200
And so it's globally available to your app at all times

04:44.200 --> 04:46.280
because this is a view component.

04:46.280 --> 04:49.480
And slot, if we think about it, it actually

04:49.480 --> 04:51.800
functions just as you would expect when it comes to HTML.

04:51.800 --> 04:52.920
You just didn't realize it.

04:52.920 --> 04:56.640
So rather than being like, oh, I wanted to say arrow and then

04:56.640 --> 04:59.880
dash hi, this is a base button.

04:59.880 --> 05:02.200
And we call it like a normal component.

05:02.200 --> 05:05.880
And we say arrow, left, and hi.

05:09.760 --> 05:12.720
And if you're looking at this going like, isn't that HTML?

05:12.720 --> 05:14.680
I'd say exactly.

05:14.680 --> 05:16.560
It's just like HTML.

05:16.560 --> 05:18.880
Why complicate things beyond that, right?

05:18.880 --> 05:21.120
There are times where you just want to let people just

05:21.120 --> 05:23.920
define whatever they want inside of your component.

05:23.920 --> 05:26.480
Now, granted, eventually you could do some fancy things

05:26.480 --> 05:27.600
regarding maybe some props.

05:27.600 --> 05:31.160
Maybe there is a point where you have some base icon here,

05:31.160 --> 05:32.160
for example.

05:32.160 --> 05:37.040
But slots are one of the most powerful features of view

05:37.040 --> 05:39.440
in the sense that it allows for flexibility.

05:39.440 --> 05:41.440
It's almost like in that regard, the complete opposite

05:41.440 --> 05:44.120
of props, where props are being very prescriptive.

05:44.120 --> 05:45.200
Do this with this type.

05:45.200 --> 05:46.080
This is required.

05:46.080 --> 05:47.400
Do it in this place.

05:47.400 --> 05:50.240
Slots is saying, all right, I'm open.

05:50.240 --> 05:54.480
Like, you can basically work with me here.

05:54.480 --> 05:57.520
And so the cool thing about slots, too,

05:57.520 --> 06:00.760
is that slots are more intelligent than a standard HTML

06:00.760 --> 06:03.360
element in that you can actually provide it

06:03.360 --> 06:05.280
a default content.

06:05.280 --> 06:07.560
So what we can do is we can say, yeah, so we'll just

06:07.560 --> 06:10.680
call it submit by default.

06:10.680 --> 06:13.360
And so when you do that, this means

06:13.360 --> 06:15.960
that when I go ahead and delete what's inside,

06:15.960 --> 06:19.400
you'll notice it automatically falls back to that content,

06:19.400 --> 06:22.280
which, again, can be very nice for just providing

06:22.280 --> 06:24.440
some conventions, especially when people start having design

06:24.440 --> 06:25.920
systems and that sort of thing.

06:25.920 --> 06:27.600
It's like, OK, most of our buttons are submit.

06:27.600 --> 06:29.920
We can go ahead and say, OK, all base buttons

06:29.920 --> 06:31.320
have a default of submit.

06:31.320 --> 06:33.760
But otherwise, the moment someone needs to override it,

06:33.760 --> 06:34.840
easy peasy.

06:34.840 --> 06:35.960
This is no longer submit.

06:35.960 --> 06:37.640
This is now cancel.

06:37.640 --> 06:42.000
As you can see, updates, just as you would expect.

06:42.000 --> 06:45.120
And you can bind that, obviously, to your data?

06:45.120 --> 06:46.360
Yep, exactly.

06:46.360 --> 06:49.160
So the question here is around, can we do more than this?

06:49.160 --> 06:51.240
Yeah, because we should treat this just

06:51.240 --> 06:54.560
like any other HTML element.

06:54.560 --> 07:02.680
So in here, we can do something like user data dot name.

07:02.680 --> 07:04.800
And there you go.

07:04.800 --> 07:05.760
It just works.

07:05.760 --> 07:07.640
That's one of the things that always excites me.

07:07.640 --> 07:11.160
When I look at how Views APIs design is extensibility

07:11.160 --> 07:13.000
on what people already understand,

07:13.000 --> 07:14.680
and then just leveraging the heck out of it.

07:14.680 --> 07:15.160
Why not?

07:15.160 --> 07:18.920
Why not sprinkle on some syntactic sugar, some superpowers

07:18.920 --> 07:20.040
that make it even easier, right?

07:20.040 --> 07:22.640
Because, again, slots inherently already

07:22.640 --> 07:24.760
work from an HTML perspective.

07:24.760 --> 07:26.600
But now you can provide default content.

07:26.600 --> 07:29.560
We can actually do if conditions for slots, too.

07:29.560 --> 07:31.360
So if you have something called direction,

07:31.360 --> 07:36.400
and let's say directions here, and we can say v if left.

07:36.400 --> 07:40.960
We could use slot for v else.

07:40.960 --> 07:44.240
And then, so let's say the default is actually this submit.

07:44.240 --> 07:46.800
But otherwise, if it has a configured prop of left,

07:46.800 --> 07:51.280
it might be icon left, whatever, cancel.

07:51.320 --> 07:54.920
And so what you get here is I'm already overriding it.

07:54.920 --> 07:57.960
But if you come in here, save, you'll

07:57.960 --> 08:00.680
see that submit is falling back as we would expect.

08:00.680 --> 08:05.560
But if we have, whoop, I think my v if direction doesn't

08:05.560 --> 08:06.640
make sense.

08:06.640 --> 08:07.720
Actually, I can just do left.

08:07.720 --> 08:08.200
It's fine.

08:08.200 --> 08:14.200
So I do left is a Boolean, and the default is false.

08:14.200 --> 08:17.200
So like that, you see submit does come up.

08:17.200 --> 08:22.400
And then we can say left is true.

08:22.400 --> 08:23.920
Icon left, cancel.

08:23.920 --> 08:28.200
So that's pretty cool, but what else can we do?

08:28.200 --> 08:31.960
There's even more, because if we think of slots

08:31.960 --> 08:34.320
like a mailbox slot, we might want

08:34.320 --> 08:37.560
to have multiple slots on a component, not just one.

08:37.560 --> 08:41.880
And so what if we were to do something like layouts?

08:41.880 --> 08:46.120
Well, if you had a layout component,

08:46.160 --> 08:48.400
let's say I'm going to do baseLayout.View.

08:51.400 --> 08:52.880
What you're probably going to end up having

08:52.880 --> 08:55.120
is you probably have some sort of div

08:55.120 --> 08:56.960
that is your class wrapper, assuming you

08:56.960 --> 08:58.640
have a design system.

08:58.640 --> 09:00.840
But then you might want to start figuring out

09:00.840 --> 09:02.200
where things are being slotted.

09:02.200 --> 09:04.960
So for example, you might have an aside here that

09:04.960 --> 09:07.720
is class like sidebar.

09:07.720 --> 09:09.120
And then you probably have somewhere

09:09.120 --> 09:11.560
where you want to render out your main content.

09:11.560 --> 09:15.960
And then you probably have your footer content.

09:15.960 --> 09:18.600
And so again, if you do this by traditional props

09:18.600 --> 09:20.440
and that kind of stuff, it gets really painful,

09:20.440 --> 09:22.520
because how do you pass components into props?

09:22.520 --> 09:24.680
Like that's just a whole messy matter.

09:24.680 --> 09:29.440
But what we can do is we can give our slots names.

09:29.440 --> 09:31.480
So this can be the sidebar slot.

09:33.920 --> 09:35.800
This can be the main slot.

09:37.120 --> 09:39.680
And this can be the footer slot.

09:42.720 --> 09:44.120
And so the way this would work is,

09:44.120 --> 09:47.760
if we're inside of our app.view, we

09:47.760 --> 09:51.000
can go ahead and import base, let's see,

09:51.000 --> 09:54.240
let me duplicate this one.

09:54.240 --> 09:59.480
And then boom, boom, base layout from base layout.

09:59.480 --> 10:03.720
We organize that to be alphabetical, great.

10:03.720 --> 10:08.720
OK, then we get our base layout.

10:08.720 --> 10:11.800
What we can do now is we can now define specific snippets

10:11.800 --> 10:14.120
of HTML to go in specific places.

10:14.120 --> 10:15.680
How do we define HTML?

10:15.680 --> 10:17.400
We have our template element.

10:17.400 --> 10:19.440
And so I know it's like, whoa, there's a template

10:19.440 --> 10:20.240
and a template.

10:20.240 --> 10:22.880
But again, if you look at MDN, template

10:22.880 --> 10:24.640
is an actual HTML element.

10:24.640 --> 10:26.480
It's a content template element.

10:26.480 --> 10:27.520
Like this is a thing.

10:27.520 --> 10:28.840
I think a lot of people at one point

10:28.840 --> 10:30.320
was like, oh, that looks like a view thing.

10:30.320 --> 10:32.440
No, this isn't the MDN docs.

10:32.440 --> 10:34.440
This is fairly standard.

10:34.440 --> 10:37.920
And so what we can do is we can say, look, this is the aside.

10:37.920 --> 10:40.320
And how do we tell the template that we want to go there?

10:40.360 --> 10:42.600
Well, we do the vslot directive.

10:42.600 --> 10:48.000
And we tell it the, I believe this is aside like this.

10:48.000 --> 10:55.560
And so if this renders correctly, base layout, oh, wait,

10:55.560 --> 10:56.680
I did not.

10:56.680 --> 10:57.960
Oh, I called it sidebar.

10:57.960 --> 10:59.840
So of course, there's nowhere to go.

10:59.840 --> 11:03.160
So I misnamed it sidebar aside.

11:03.160 --> 11:04.400
There we go.

11:04.400 --> 11:06.440
And so let's give this just a little bit of styling

11:06.440 --> 11:08.520
just so that we can see the difference.

11:08.520 --> 11:15.440
So let's go ahead and give the sidebar border 2px solid red.

11:15.440 --> 11:18.240
And then we'll go ahead and do the same for each one.

11:18.240 --> 11:19.840
So we'll have main.

11:19.840 --> 11:22.840
And then we'll have footer.

11:22.840 --> 11:24.120
And so we'll make this green.

11:24.120 --> 11:26.800
We'll make this blue.

11:26.800 --> 11:28.520
And so you can see right now, because nothing's

11:28.520 --> 11:31.120
being passed to the slot for main or footer,

11:31.120 --> 11:33.800
nothing's being rendered, just the aside.

11:33.800 --> 11:37.560
And so all we got to do now is we can go ahead and add things

11:37.560 --> 11:41.520
like template vslot main.

11:41.520 --> 11:44.000
And then I can even do things like actually render the user

11:44.000 --> 11:44.400
card.

11:44.400 --> 11:47.320
Actually, I could even just take this component right here.

11:47.320 --> 11:51.360
Let's take the user card here and just drop it directly

11:51.360 --> 11:52.200
inside of main.

11:55.640 --> 11:58.000
And so you can see here that it's actually being rendered

11:58.000 --> 12:01.880
inside, although where did my borders go?

12:01.880 --> 12:03.520
Main class.

12:03.520 --> 12:05.200
Oh, I didn't give it a class.

12:05.200 --> 12:06.320
Class main.

12:06.320 --> 12:08.200
There we go.

12:08.200 --> 12:09.600
Did you have a question?

12:09.600 --> 12:13.240
I was wondering if the components that are not the slots

12:13.240 --> 12:18.200
render in the DOM when you bring that template in.

12:18.200 --> 12:22.200
So you're saying that if I don't.

12:22.200 --> 12:24.880
So currently you have nothing in the footer slot.

12:24.880 --> 12:25.380
Yep.

12:25.380 --> 12:26.880
Is there a footer element?

12:26.880 --> 12:29.280
Ah, good question.

12:29.280 --> 12:33.720
And the answer is yes, it is there.

12:33.720 --> 12:35.560
Because as far as view is concerned, what you're saying

12:35.560 --> 12:39.320
is this is the scaffold expected to render.

12:39.320 --> 12:41.640
There's nothing.

12:41.640 --> 12:46.240
Well, yeah, I guess it would be a little bit tricky

12:46.240 --> 12:48.760
as far as checking to make sure the slot, whether the slot is

12:48.760 --> 12:49.720
empty or not.

12:49.720 --> 12:52.080
So at that point, though, I'm saying that I would say

12:52.080 --> 12:53.760
that if you're starting to get conditional about what

12:53.760 --> 12:56.080
content is showing inside, at that point,

12:56.080 --> 12:59.320
it would make more sense to make a slot name of a slot of

12:59.320 --> 13:00.280
footer, for example.

13:01.280 --> 13:07.360
And then maybe the default is to have some content in here,

13:07.360 --> 13:09.440
but then otherwise let someone completely override it.

13:09.440 --> 13:12.160
And then that way, if there is no slot, nothing shows up at

13:12.160 --> 13:13.000
all.

13:13.000 --> 13:14.800
That's probably the way I would approach it in that

13:14.800 --> 13:17.680
particular case.

13:17.680 --> 13:18.240
So here we go.

13:18.240 --> 13:19.080
We have that.

13:19.080 --> 13:20.520
Whoops, what did I forget?

13:20.520 --> 13:21.600
There we go.

13:21.600 --> 13:22.240
Boom.

13:22.240 --> 13:25.600
So we can see now the footer actually is rendering.

13:25.600 --> 13:29.480
And so all I've got to do now is do the template vslot

13:29.480 --> 13:31.560
footer inside of here.

13:31.560 --> 13:33.160
And then let's drop the base counter in there.

13:38.160 --> 13:40.400
And so slots become really powerful, because if you've

13:40.400 --> 13:43.800
ever worked with a backend CMS where users are defining

13:43.800 --> 13:46.920
layouts, now you have the ability to, when you're fetching

13:46.920 --> 13:49.680
data, to actually drop things where they're supposed to go

13:49.680 --> 13:52.520
as opposed to having to configure like, oh, this is my

13:52.520 --> 13:53.600
two-column layout.

13:53.600 --> 13:55.000
This is my three-column layout.

13:55.000 --> 13:56.800
This is my two-column with one.

13:56.800 --> 13:59.320
There comes a point where that becomes very tedious to

13:59.320 --> 14:00.080
maintain.

14:00.080 --> 14:02.920
And slots do a lot in terms of opening up a lot of

14:02.920 --> 14:05.560
reusability and composability when it comes to that.

14:05.560 --> 14:07.120
What's cool about this, too, is that because it's

14:07.120 --> 14:12.120
programmatic in terms of assigning its property,

14:12.120 --> 14:14.040
it doesn't have to appear in a certain order.

14:14.040 --> 14:18.360
So for example, I'll just swap these to show that.

14:18.360 --> 14:21.640
You'll see that even though technically inside of here we've

14:21.640 --> 14:25.000
ordered it as a side, main, whatever, it's totally being

14:25.000 --> 14:27.360
controlled at this level.

14:27.360 --> 14:30.360
And so as a result, you can, again, it just allows for more

14:30.360 --> 14:34.680
composability and flexibility when it comes to that.

14:34.680 --> 14:36.600
And the other thing I want to leave you off with before we

14:36.600 --> 14:39.600
dive into exercises and other questions is that if you're

14:39.600 --> 14:42.280
looking at some other code bases, not everyone writes out

14:42.280 --> 14:44.560
the vslot directive.

14:44.560 --> 14:47.520
And this is a convention within view three in that you can

14:47.520 --> 14:51.440
shorthand it to just the hashtag symbol.

14:51.440 --> 14:53.880
The way to think about it is like the idea of the slot that

14:53.880 --> 14:56.680
you want to put the template stuff in.

14:56.680 --> 14:59.960
To be honest, I find it a little bit confusing because if

14:59.960 --> 15:02.520
someone doesn't know what this is and tries to Google this,

15:02.520 --> 15:04.920
sometimes they might end up with CSS stuff.

15:04.920 --> 15:08.480
This shorthand, personally, I don't tend to use it.

